        "transcriptText": "Editorial reading copy of the teacher-supplied transcript. Names, obvious recognition errors and punctuation have been corrected. Spoken grammar is retained. This has not been checked word for word against the audio; unclear passages are marked rather than guessed. Times are approximate.\n\n[00:00] Presenter: The England and Manchester United footballer Marcus Rashford has been speaking about his own experiences of poverty and being short of food as a child, as part of his campaign for the government to provide free school meal vouchers for vulnerable children throughout the school holidays. He’s written to every MP for support after helping to raise £20 million for food for low-income families during the lockdown. Our education correspondent Elaine Dunkley reports.\n\n[About 00:23] Reporter: He’s a hero on the pitch and one of Manchester United’s highest-earning players, but Marcus Rashford hasn’t forgotten his roots. He has raised £20 million to supply meals to families in what he calls the pandemic of food poverty. In an emotional letter, he describes how his mother, a single parent, struggled to feed her five children while earning the minimum wage.\n\n[About 00:47] Rashford: You know, my mum, she done the best she could. I remember we used to go to a shop called Poundworld and everything was under a pound. And, you know, we sort of scheduled out the week, so we’d get seven yoghurts and you can have one yoghurt a day. And this is all going on at a time when kids should be concentrating on schoolwork. And it’s just crazy to think that this is still going on at this—you know, we’re in 2020 now—and it’s just something that I don’t believe should be happening now.\n\n[About 01:12] Reporter: Marcus Rashford grew up in Wythenshawe, on what was once thought to be the largest council estate in Europe. He went to this primary school—\n\nSchool representative: He’s a real hero to our children.\n\nReporter: —where the number of children receiving free school meals is twice the national average.\n\n[About 01:29] School representative: Families may have unemployment that’s come as a result of COVID-19. They may well be on reduced hours, so, you know, financially there’s been a strain. So what Marcus is saying is, you know, really quite a small ask, I think.\n\n[About 01:44] Reporter: In the local shopping precinct, I met Gemma. Both her children are among the 1.3 million youngsters in England who receive free school meals. She says keeping them fed is a constant struggle.\n\n[About 02:00] Gemma: Try living our life for a week. It’s not fun, because I wish—I wish we all knew [unclear: words about when they can next buy food] or what’s going to happen.\n\n[About 02:10] Reporter: Whether in work or out of work, families here are feeling the pressure. The government has confirmed that the national voucher scheme will not run this summer, but says £63 million has been allocated to local authorities to help those who are struggling. Marcus Rashford hopes the government will change its mind and find what’s believed to be around £120 million to fund the voucher scheme over the summer holidays.\n\n[About 02:32] Rashford: Well, basically, I’m just hoping that the government make a—make a U-turn on the decision to stop the free meal vouchers. And I’m just hoping to do it as soon as possible. [Unclear short phrase.] They’ve mentioned that they usually do this, you know, this time of year, summer holidays. But because of COVID, the situation’s been completely different for everyone in the world. So, you know, circumstances change. I think they have to be willing to make that decision to go back on themselves.\n\n[About 03:06] Presenter: Marcus Rashford ending that report from Elaine Dunkley.\n\nContext note: The report describes the position before the summer-fund announcement. Resource 2 reports the later change. Spoken forms such as “she done” belong to the interview and are not models of formal written grammar.",
